    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "body": "I'd prefer torch-like semantics.\r\n\r\nI originally was rewriting this as `tl.exp(tl.log(x) * y)`, though one gotcha is handling of negatives and integer exponents.  Here are a few examples I experimented with:\r\n\r\n`x**2` requires `torch.exp(torch.log(torch.abs(x))*y)`\r\n```\r\n>>> x\r\ntensor([ 0.9814,  0.1270, -1.6873,  2.1067,  1.5667, -1.1224,  1.8748,  1.8345])\r\n>>> y\r\ntensor([2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2])\r\n>>> torch.pow(x,y)\r\ntensor([0.9631, 0.0161, 2.8471, 4.4380, 2.4544, 1.2598, 3.5149, 3.3655])\r\n>>> torch.exp(torch.log(x)*y)\r\ntensor([0.9631, 0.0161,    nan, 4.4380, 2.4544,    nan, 3.5149, 3.3655])\r\n>>> torch.exp(torch.log(torch.abs(x))*y)\r\ntensor([0.9631, 0.0161, 2.8471, 4.4380, 2.4544, 1.2598, 3.5149, 3.3655])\r\n>>> \r\n````\r\n\r\n`x**3.0` requires `torch.sign(x)*torch.exp(torch.log(torch.abs(x))*y)`\r\n```\r\n>>> y=torch.tensor([3.0]*8)\r\n>>> torch.pow(x,y)\r\ntensor([ 9.4517e-01,  2.0479e-03, -4.8040e+00,  9.3493e+00,  3.8453e+00,\r\n        -1.4140e+00,  6.5897e+00,  6.1741e+00])\r\n>>> torch.exp(torch.log(x)*y)\r\ntensor([9.4517e-01, 2.0479e-03,        nan, 9.3493e+00, 3.8453e+00,        nan,\r\n        6.5897e+00, 6.1741e+00])\r\n>>> torch.sign(x)*torch.exp(torch.log(torch.abs(x))*y)\r\ntensor([ 9.4517e-01,  2.0479e-03, -4.8040e+00,  9.3493e+00,  3.8453e+00,\r\n        -1.4140e+00,  6.5897e+00,  6.1741e+00])\r\n```\r\n\r\n`x**1.5` works with `torch.exp(torch.log(x)*y)`\r\n```\r\n>>> y=torch.tensor([1.5]*8)\r\n>>> torch.pow(x,y)\r\ntensor([0.9722, 0.0453,    nan, 3.0577, 1.9609,    nan, 2.5670, 2.4848])\r\n>>> torch.exp(torch.log(x)*y)\r\ntensor([0.9722, 0.0453,    nan, 3.0577, 1.9609,    nan, 2.5670, 2.4848])\r\n```\r\n\r\nSo there are at least 3 cases:\r\n- Even integer (even floats that are integers count here)\r\n- Odd integer\r\n- Not integer\r\n\r\nThere may be more I haven't thought of...\r\n\r\nSo things started spiraling a bit into a mess.  I was hoping there was some better way to solve this in assembly...\r\n",
